  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q4-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Let $f:[a,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ where $f(x)=x^2+4x-5$. Find the least value of $a$ for which $f$ is a strictly increasing function.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=-2$.\n\nThe function is strictly increasing on $[-2,\\infty)$; any smaller starting value includes a decreasing interval.",
      "worked": "For $-2\\le u<v$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(v)-f(u)&=v^2+4v-5-(u^2+4u-5)\\\\f(v)-f(u)&=(v-u)(v+u+4).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors are positive: $v-u>0$ and $v+u+4>0$. Hence $f(u)<f(v)$, so $f$ is strictly increasing on $[-2,\\infty)$.\n\nIf $a<-2$, then $a<-2$ but\n$$\\begin{align*}f(a)&=(a+2)^2-9\\\\f(a)&>-9=f(-2).\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradicts strict increase on $[a,\\infty)$. Therefore the least value is $a=-2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
